/*
 * EXEMPLAR — Form B: transactional outbox + relay.
 * The counterpart of `KafkaProducerAdapter.java.example` (Form A, publish after commit).
 * Which form applies is decided in `SKILL.md` step 3, first axis, against the criterion in
 * `.claude/rules/messaging.md` § Publication timing. Form A is the default; this file is
 * for the case where losing the event between the commit and the publish is not acceptable.
 * Form reference for the executor agent; not a file to copy verbatim.
 * Rules: `.claude/rules/messaging.md` (§ Publication timing, § Delivery semantics)
 *        `.claude/rules/architecture-ddd.md` (Adapters, Application)
 *        `.claude/rules/persistence.md` (the table itself)
 *        `.claude/rules/observability.md` (§ Metrics — the dead-letter counter and the
 *        pending-age gauge are required, not decoration; `OutboxRelayJob` records them)
 *        `.claude/rules/scheduling.md` (the trigger — `jobs-architect`'s)
 *
 * WHAT THIS EXEMPLAR SHOWS:
 *  - the outbound port's implementation writes a row instead of sending: it runs inside the
 *    caller's transaction, so state and intent-to-publish commit or roll back together;
 *  - a relay PASS — claim, send, mark, dead-letter — is a use case: `RelayOutboxEventsService`
 *    behind the inbound port `RelayOutboxEvents`. The broker is reached through the outbound
 *    port `OutboxEventSender`, and `KafkaOutboxEventSender` is the only class in Form B that
 *    imports `KafkaTemplate`;
 *  - the pass reaches outbox state through `OutboxRelayGateway`, an application-layer
 *    abstraction — never through the persistence adapter's JPA entity or Spring Data
 *    repository, which live in a sibling adapter it may not import;
 *  - duplicates are expected by design (send succeeds, mark fails) and are absorbed by the
 *    idempotent consumer the rule already requires;
 *  - the pass returns what it did (`RelayOutcome`), so whoever runs it can count dead letters
 *    without the application layer registering a meter;
 *  - the AsyncAPI contract is annotated on the WRITER's `publish`, the class that turns the payload
 *    into JSON text — never on `KafkaOutboxEventSender`, which moves text already written and knows
 *    no payload type (`.claude/rules/messaging.md` § Contract documentation). Its `TOPIC` is the
 *    value `TopicResolver` maps `OrderConfirmed` to: one decision in two files, which review holds
 *    together because the resolver is shared infrastructure and cannot import a flow's constant.
 *
 * SCHEDULING IS NOT HERE. When a pass runs, how often, on which instances, and the switch that
 * keeps it out of the integration tests belong to `jobs-architect` and its `35-jobs.md`:
 * `jobs-architect/templates/OutboxRelayJob.java.example` is the trigger — `@Scheduled`, fixed
 * delay, gated by `app.outbox.enabled`, `false` in the test profile — and it records the two
 * delivery metrics this form requires (`outbox.events.dead_lettered`,
 * `outbox.pending.age.seconds`) from each pass. Their names and the guarantee they watch stay
 * this skill's; the trigger is only where every pass goes through. Until decision 0087 the
 * `@Scheduled` method lived inside the messaging adapter, so the schedule, the coordination
 * across instances and the broker code were one class — and the prune job had nowhere to live
 * and was handed to nobody (lessons-learned-014 § 9). An ungated relay under `*IT` is still the
 * failure to remember: it polled the test database, blocked on a broker no test starts, and
 * marked rows under the tests asserting on them (lessons-learned-013 § 3).
 *
 * SCHEMA REQUIREMENT, NOT DDL. `outbox_events` is shared by the whole project — one table,
 * not one per event, same nature as `idempotency_keys`. `persistence-architect` models it,
 * from the requirements the messaging partial's § 2 states:
 *   id · aggregate id (partition key source) · event type · payload · occurred at
 *   published at (null = pending)  · attempts  · last error  · dead lettered
 *   an index that serves the claim query alone — pending rows only, not the whole table
 * Writing the DDL here would put schema in two owners' hands; see `.claude/rules/persistence.md`.
 * That skill's step 4b models it from `persistence-architect/templates/OutboxEventTable.sql.example`
 * and `OutboxEventStore.java.example` — the second implements `OutboxRelayGateway` below, and
 * this file stays the port's only declaration.
 *
 * ONE CARRIER, BOTH DIRECTIONS. `OutboxEventRecord` below is what the appender writes and
 * what the relay reads. A run that invents a second, identical record for the write side
 * (`NewOutboxEvent` next to `PendingOutboxEvent`, same five components in the same order) has
 * written the same type twice — lessons-learned-013 § 8.3. When one direction genuinely gains
 * a component the other cannot carry, that is the moment the type splits, and the partial says
 * so; until then there is one.
 *
 * WHAT'S NOT HERE: the relay's trigger and the pruning job for published rows —
 * `jobs-architect/templates/OutboxRelayJob.java.example` and `OutboxPruneJob.java.example`,
 * no messaging decision in either — and retry of the broker send itself, which is the producer
 * client's (`application-kafka.yml.example`) plus the row's own attempt count below. A
 * `DefaultErrorHandler` is a consumer's (`KafkaListenerErrorHandler.java.example`), never the
 * relay's.
 *
 * ONE SERIALIZATION, AT APPEND TIME. `OutboxAppender` turns the payload record into JSON text
 * with Boot's `JsonMapper` — the same conversion the Form A publisher does before `send` — and
 * that text is the row's `payload`. From there on it is only carried: `KafkaOutboxEventSender`
 * sends it as it is, through a `StringSerializer` (`application-kafka.yml.example`). A JSON
 * value serializer at this point would encode the text a second time, as a quoted string, with
 * a `__TypeId__` header of `java.lang.String` (issue #58). The relay never learns any flow's
 * payload class, which is what keeps those classes package-private.
 *
 * ELIDED, adapter-local and uninteresting: `OutboxAppender` (serializes the payload record
 * and inserts the row through the same outbound abstraction, in `…messaging.outbox` —
 * every flow appends through it, so it is shared infrastructure and `public`),
 * `TopicResolver` (event type to topic name, in `…messaging.outbox` for the same reason, per
 * `.claude/rules/messaging.md` § Topics and serialization), and `OrderConfirmedPayload` —
 * unchanged from Form A, in this flow's own `…messaging.orderconfirmed`, see
 * `KafkaProducerAdapter.java.example`. The payload shape does not depend on the publication
 * form. Those two are the exception the § Boundary rule allows by name: package-private is the
 * default, and a class another subpackage genuinely consumes opens up one at a time.
 *
 * PACKAGE: the outbox is shared transport infrastructure, not one event's flow, and that is
 * exactly why it gets its own subpackage instead of being dropped into the adapter root —
 * `…messaging.outbox` (`.claude/rules/messaging.md` § Boundary). A root that holds "whatever
 * belongs to nobody" is the flat package the rule exists to prevent. Its persistence half
 * lives under `…persistence.outbox` for the same reason, named as a pair on purpose: one
 * concern, two adapters, one subpackage name in each
 * (`persistence-architect/templates/OutboxEventStore.java.example`). The event's own payload
 * and its writer stay in the flow's subpackage; only what every event's relay shares lives
 * here. Package-private by default, as everywhere else in this adapter.
 *
 * VOCABULARY. Names and packages here follow the hexagonal blueprint — an inbound port
 * interface plus a `…Service`. The active blueprint's naming convention wins: in the
 * clean-architecture blueprints the pass is one concrete `RelayOutboxEventsUseCase` in
 * `application.usecase.outbox`, no interface, and the relay job depends on that class. Same shape,
 * same annotations, one type fewer — and never both vocabularies in one project.
 *
 * WIRING. The pass is a use case like any other, so it is wired like one — `@Service` in the
 * application layer, exactly as `domain-modeling/templates/UseCasePort.java.example` does.
 * Its two numbers arrive with each call, as `RelayOutboxEventsCommand` — the use case's input,
 * built by the relay job from `app.outbox.*` (`jobs-architect/templates/OutboxRelayJob.java.example`).
 * Not a settings bean injected at construction: no blueprint names that role, and the
 * clean-architecture convention already has one for a use case's input — `Command`, next to its
 * `UseCase` (lessons-learned-016 § 11). No `@Configuration` anywhere builds the service by hand.
 *
 * Each `// --- <dir>/<File>.java` block is its own file, at that subpackage.
 *
 * The sender binds nothing of `app.outbox.*`. `batch-size` and `max-attempts` are bound by
 * the relay job, which builds `RelayOutboxEventsCommand` from them on every pass —
 * `jobs-architect/templates/OutboxRelayJob.java.example`. No `@Configuration` here turns
 * them into a bean for the use case.
 *
 * TESTS (`40-tests.md`), none of them deferrable, all without a broker:
 *  - `RelayOutboxEventsServiceTest` — unit, fake gateway and sender: the SENT, FAILED and
 *    UNROUTABLE paths, the FAILED row that exhausts its attempts, and one failing row not
 *    stopping the rest of the batch;
 *  - `KafkaOutboxEventSenderTest` — unit, mocked `KafkaTemplate`: the three results.
 * The broker-level test may be deferred by `40-tests.md`; these may not. Relay coverage that
 * comes only from a scheduler having run during the integration tests is accidental
 * (lessons-learned-013 § 8.1).
 */

// --- OutboxRelayGateway.java (application layer — the relay's only view of outbox state)
package com.example.demo.application.port.out;

import java.time.Instant;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;
import java.util.UUID;

public interface OutboxRelayGateway {

    /** Pending rows, oldest first, capped. Ordered per aggregate so partition order holds. */
    List<OutboxEventRecord> claimPending(int batchSize);

    void markPublished(UUID eventId);

    /**
     * Increments attempts; flags the row dead-lettered once maxAttempts is reached. Returns
     * whether THIS call is the one that dead-lettered it — the relay cannot see the row's
     * attempts count, and a counter that misses the main loss path measures nothing.
     */
    boolean markFailed(UUID eventId, String reason, int maxAttempts);

    /**
     * Flags the row dead-lettered now, without spending attempts: a failure that retrying
     * cannot fix, such as an event type no topic maps. Says what it means — passing
     * `maxAttempts = 0` to `markFailed` reaches the same state by arithmetic and reads as a
     * sentinel nobody can explain six months later (lessons-learned-013 § 8.4).
     */
    void markDeadLettered(UUID eventId, String reason);

    /**
     * When the oldest still-pending row occurred, or empty when nothing is pending. Feeds the
     * gauge below: a counter says loss already happened, this says delivery is falling behind
     * before it does.
     */
    Optional<Instant> oldestPendingOccurredAt();
}

// --- OutboxEventRecord.java (application layer — carries no JPA and no Kafka type)
package com.example.demo.application.port.out;

import java.time.Instant;
import java.util.UUID;

public record OutboxEventRecord(
        UUID eventId, String aggregateId, String eventType, String payload, Instant occurredAt) {}

// --- orderconfirmed/OrderConfirmedOutboxWriter.java (implements the port domain-modeling declared)
package com.example.demo.adapter.out.messaging.orderconfirmed;

import com.example.demo.application.port.out.PublishOrderConfirmedPort;
import com.example.demo.domain.event.OrderConfirmed;
import io.github.springwolf.core.asyncapi.annotations.AsyncMessage;
import io.github.springwolf.core.asyncapi.annotations.AsyncOperation;
import io.github.springwolf.core.asyncapi.annotations.AsyncPublisher;
import lombok.AccessLevel;
import lombok.RequiredArgsConstructor;
import lombok.experimental.FieldDefaults;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

/**
 * No `@Transactional` here: the use case owns the transaction
 * (`.claude/rules/architecture-ddd.md` § Application). This class only has to run inside it —
 * writing the row in its own transaction would recreate exactly the gap Form B closes.
 */
@Component
@RequiredArgsConstructor
@FieldDefaults(level = AccessLevel.PRIVATE, makeFinal = true)
public class OrderConfirmedOutboxWriter implements PublishOrderConfirmedPort {

    private static final String TOPIC = "orders.order.confirmed";

    OutboxAppender outbox;

    @Override
    @AsyncPublisher(
            operation =
                    @AsyncOperation(
                            channelName = TOPIC,
                            description =
                                    "Emitted once an order is confirmed. Partition key: orderId (string), so every"
                                            + " event of one order stays in order.",
                            payloadType = OrderConfirmedPayload.class,
                            message = @AsyncMessage(messageId = "OrderConfirmed", name = "OrderConfirmed"),
                            headers = @AsyncOperation.Headers(notUsed = true)))
    public void publish(OrderConfirmed event) {
        outbox.append(
                event.orderId().value().toString(),
                "OrderConfirmed",
                OrderConfirmedPayload.from(event));
    }
}

// --- RelayOutboxEvents.java (application layer — the inbound port the relay job calls)
package com.example.demo.application.port.in.outbox;

import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.Optional;

public interface RelayOutboxEvents {

    /** One pass: claim a batch, send each row, mark it. Never throws for a single row's failure. */
    RelayOutcome relayPending(RelayOutboxEventsCommand command);

    /** How far behind delivery is — empty when nothing is pending. Feeds the pending-age gauge. */
    Optional<Duration> oldestPendingAge();

    record RelayOutcome(int published, int failed, int deadLettered) {

        public static final RelayOutcome NONE = new RelayOutcome(0, 0, 0);
        public static final RelayOutcome PUBLISHED_ONE = new RelayOutcome(1, 0, 0);
        public static final RelayOutcome FAILED_ONE = new RelayOutcome(0, 1, 0);
        public static final RelayOutcome DEAD_LETTERED_ONE = new RelayOutcome(0, 0, 1);

        public RelayOutcome plus(RelayOutcome other) {
            return new RelayOutcome(
                    published + other.published, failed + other.failed, deadLettered + other.deadLettered);
        }
    }
}

// --- OutboxEventSender.java (application layer — the relay's only view of the broker)
package com.example.demo.application.port.out;

public interface OutboxEventSender {

    /**
     * Three outcomes, and the relay treats each differently: a sent row is marked published, a
     * failed one spends an attempt, an unroutable one is dead-lettered now — no retry will map a
     * type nobody registered. Nothing Kafka-typed crosses this signature.
     */
    SendResult send(OutboxEventRecord event);

    enum Status { SENT, FAILED, UNROUTABLE }

    record SendResult(Status status, String reason) {

        public static SendResult sent() {
            return new SendResult(Status.SENT, null);
        }
    }
}

// --- RelayOutboxEventsCommand.java (application layer — one pass's input, no framework type)
package com.example.demo.application.port.in.outbox;

/**
 * Values are `20-persistence.md`'s; the relay job binds them from `app.outbox.*` and passes
 * them on every call. Next to the inbound port here; in the clean-architecture blueprints, next
 * to `RelayOutboxEventsUseCase` in `application.usecase.outbox`, as that convention places every
 * Command.
 */
public record RelayOutboxEventsCommand(int batchSize, int maxAttempts) {

    public RelayOutboxEventsCommand {
        if (batchSize <= 0 || maxAttempts <= 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("batchSize and maxAttempts must be positive");
        }
    }
}

// --- RelayOutboxEventsService.java (application layer — one pass; no scheduling, no Kafka)
package com.example.demo.application.service.outbox;

import com.example.demo.application.port.in.outbox.RelayOutboxEvents;
import com.example.demo.application.port.in.outbox.RelayOutboxEventsCommand;
import com.example.demo.application.port.out.OutboxEventRecord;
import com.example.demo.application.port.out.OutboxEventSender;
import com.example.demo.application.port.out.OutboxEventSender.SendResult;
import com.example.demo.application.port.out.OutboxRelayGateway;
import java.time.Clock;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.Optional;
import lombok.AccessLevel;
import lombok.RequiredArgsConstructor;
import lombok.experimental.FieldDefaults;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

/**
 * No `@Transactional`: claim, send and mark are three separate units by design — the gateway
 * opens one per mark (`OutboxEventStore.java.example`), and holding a transaction across a
 * broker send is the long transaction Form B exists to avoid.
 */
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
@FieldDefaults(level = AccessLevel.PRIVATE, makeFinal = true)
public class RelayOutboxEventsService implements RelayOutboxEvents {

    OutboxRelayGateway gateway;
    OutboxEventSender sender;
    Clock clock;

    /**
     * Dead-lettered by name, not by an attempts count that happens to arrive at
     * the same state (lessons-learned-013 § 8.4).
     *
     * The send may well have succeeded; the consumer's dedupe is what makes the
     * retry safe. One poisoned row must not stall the batch.
     */
    @Override
    public RelayOutcome relayPending(RelayOutboxEventsCommand command) {
        RelayOutcome outcome = RelayOutcome.NONE;
        for (OutboxEventRecord event : gateway.claimPending(command.batchSize())) {
            outcome = outcome.plus(relay(event, command.maxAttempts()));
        }
        return outcome;
    }

    /** The branch computes one row's outcome, so it is a switch expression (code-quality.md). */
    private RelayOutcome relay(OutboxEventRecord event, int maxAttempts) {
        SendResult result = sender.send(event);
        return switch (result.status()) {
            case SENT -> publish(event);
            case UNROUTABLE -> deadLetter(event, result.reason());
            case FAILED -> fail(event, result.reason(), maxAttempts);
        };
    }

    private RelayOutcome publish(OutboxEventRecord event) {
        gateway.markPublished(event.eventId());
        return RelayOutcome.PUBLISHED_ONE;
    }

    private RelayOutcome deadLetter(OutboxEventRecord event, String reason) {
        gateway.markDeadLettered(event.eventId(), reason);
        return RelayOutcome.DEAD_LETTERED_ONE;
    }

    private RelayOutcome fail(OutboxEventRecord event, String reason, int maxAttempts) {
        return gateway.markFailed(event.eventId(), reason, maxAttempts)
                ? RelayOutcome.DEAD_LETTERED_ONE
                : RelayOutcome.FAILED_ONE;
    }

    @Override
    public Optional<Duration> oldestPendingAge() {
        return gateway.oldestPendingOccurredAt().map(occurredAt -> Duration.between(occurredAt, clock.instant()));
    }
}

// --- outbox/KafkaOutboxEventSender.java (the only class in Form B that imports KafkaTemplate)
package com.example.demo.adapter.out.messaging.outbox;

import com.example.demo.application.port.out.OutboxEventRecord;
import com.example.demo.application.port.out.OutboxEventSender;
import java.util.concurrent.CompletionException;
import lombok.AccessLevel;
import lombok.RequiredArgsConstructor;
import lombok.experimental.FieldDefaults;
import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
@RequiredArgsConstructor(access = AccessLevel.PACKAGE)
@FieldDefaults(level = AccessLevel.PRIVATE, makeFinal = true)
class KafkaOutboxEventSender implements OutboxEventSender {

    KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate;
    TopicResolver topics;

    /**
     * Partition key = aggregate id, so per-aggregate order holds (`messaging.md`).
     *
     * `join()` wraps every send failure in this one type, which is also why the catch is
     * not `RuntimeException`: Checkstyle's IllegalCatch rejects that, and the narrower
     * type is what the call actually throws.
     */
    @Override
    public SendResult send(OutboxEventRecord event) {
        String topic = topics.forEventType(event.eventType());
        if (topic == null) {
            return new SendResult(Status.UNROUTABLE, "no topic mapped for " + event.eventType());
        }
        try {
            kafkaTemplate.send(topic, event.aggregateId(), event.payload()).join();
            return SendResult.sent();
        } catch (CompletionException e) {
            return new SendResult(Status.FAILED, e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
